When $\Qcal$ refines $\Pcal$, the first map resolves each coarse coordinate into its children and the second adds the children back to their parent.
\end{definition}

\begin{keyidea}
A coarse coordinate retains a whole vector in its coarse packet. Adding its children keeps the whole vector inside that packet. Fine analysis recovers each child by applying its projector. This explains why the two coordinate maps can be inverse even when the fine system has more packet labels.
\end{keyidea}

\begin{interpretation}
These identities concern complete coordinates of the same module. A quotient is a different operation: it identifies vectors that differ by an element of a chosen submodule. Later, finite precision will be expressed by a quotient that removes terms beyond the chosen filtration depth. This distinction explains why refinement is exactly reversible while precision truncation has a prescribed residual.
\end{interpretation}

This is why the truncation rule uses total weight rather than path length. The lower bound in \cref{cor:minimum_path_weight} need not be an equality because several paths of the same weight may cancel.
\end{example}

\begin{keyidea}
Word length counts how many transfers are composed. Weight counts how much filtration gain those transfers guarantee. The sign in the inverse expansion depends on length, while the precision cutoff depends on weight. A short word can therefore disappear at a chosen precision while a longer word still contributes.
\end{keyidea}

Positive weights also imply
$|\gamma|\le W(\gamma)$, making the retained search finite.

\begin{interpretation}
Using the standard length cutoff, every discarded word has weight at least the chosen depth and vanishes in the quotient. The retained sum therefore satisfies both inverse identities exactly at that precision. The earlier truncation theorem states the corresponding residual bounds in the complete filtered algebra itself.
\end{interpretation}
